! The collinear counterpart of `h-noncolin-ultracell.in`: the same simple-cubic
! hydrogen lattice at `nspin = 2`, which is the cell a *seeded* modulation is
! demonstrated on.
!
! **The lattice constant is chosen and neither end of its range works.** At
! `a = 5.0` the moment is 0.027 and the cell sits on the Stoner threshold, where
! the moment is the difference of two large numbers; at `a = 6.0` it is 0.9997,
! a saturated atom whose `|zeta| = 1` is the clamp-tangent trap. At 5.5 with a
! 0.8 seed it converges in six iterations to 0.62 and responds to everything.
!
! **The k-grid is part of that statement.** The moment is 0.6234 on this
! `4 2 2` grid and on nothing else -- `4 1 1` gives 1.0000, a saturated atom,
! `4 4 4` gives 0.7901 and `6 6 6` gives 0.8020 -- so quote the grid with the
! number or it is a different cell. `4 2 2` is what a `(2, 1, 1)` ultracell on a
! `(2, 2, 2)` mesh of its own zone folds to.
!
! `nosym` because an ultracell needs it: a modulation breaks the crystal's point
! group.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
 /
 &system
    ibrav = 1, celldm(1) = 5.5, nat = 1, ntyp = 1, ecutwfc = 15.0,
    nosym = .true., noinv = .true.,
    nspin = 2, starting_magnetization(1) = 0.8,
    occupations = 'smearing', smearing = 'gaussian', degauss = 0.02
 /
 &electrons
    conv_thr = 1.0d-11
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 H  1.008  H.pz-vbc.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS crystal
 H 0.0 0.0 0.0
K_POINTS automatic
 4 2 2 0 0 0
